{"text": "Report of Progress~Grant 2489A\n\nElliott Sigal, P.l.\n\n15-Lipoxygenase is a highly regulated lipid peroxidating enzyme that was recently\nimplicated in the pathogenesis of atherosclerosis and asthma. This enzyme has a specific\ntissue distribution that includes airway epithelial cells, eosinophils, macrophages in\natherosclerotic lesions and reticulocytes.\n\nThe expression of 15-lipoxygenase in atherosclerotic lesions indicate that the\nenzyme is induced in the atherosclerotic lesions because the precursor to the foamy\nmacrophage- the peripheral blood monocyte- does not express this enzyme. Because\ncytokines mediate many of the cellular responses seen in atherosclerosis, we searched for\ncytokines that could regulate 15-lipoxygenase expression in cultured peripheral blood\nmonocytes. Our studies showed that 15-lipoxygenase expression was induced by\ninterleukin-4 and that this induction was inhibited by hydrocortisone, yinterferon and\nphorbol esters.\n\nAlthough it remains unclear if IL4 stimulates 15-lipoxygenase expression in\natherosclerotic lesions, a similar induction occurs in airway epithelial cells. 15-\nLipoxygenase is a major arachidonic acid metabolizing enzyme in airway epithelial cells.\nWhen human pulmonary tissue is incubated with arachidonic acid, large amounts of 15S-\nhydroxyeicosatetraenoic acid (15S-HETE), the major arachidonic acid metabolite of 15-\nlipoxygenase, is produced along with smaller amounts of 8,15-diHETE, 14,15-diHETE, and\n12S-HETE. Immunohistochemical studies localize 15-lipoxygenase to the ciliated airway\nepithelial cells and its expression extends from the upper airways to the respiratory\nepithelium. Increased amounts of 15-HETE are found in bronchoalveolar lavage of patients\nwith asthma and more recent data suggest that 15-lipoxygenase protein levels are\nupregulated in the airway epithelial cells of asthmatics. Arachidonic acid metabolites of\n15-lipoxygenase can contribute to mucus secretion, alter epithelial ion transport and\nstimulate neutrophil and eosinophil chemotaxis. The sequential actions of 15- and 5-\nlipoxygenases generate lipoxins A and B which can affect pulmonary physiology by\nstimulating neutrophil degranulation, activating protein kinase C, and altering chloride ion\ntransport. 15-Lipoxygenase may also exert its effects directly by augmenting host defense\nmechanisms through the generation of toxic lipid peroxides in epithelial cell membranes.\n\nMonocyte Expression. Expression of 15-lipoxygenase in foamy macrophages\nof atherosclerotic lesions prompted a search for factors controlling expression of the\nenzyme in cultured monocytes. Peripheral blood monocytes were isolated from buffy coats\nby density centrifugation and monocyte adherence. Each buffy coat typically yields 15-20\nmillion monocytes with >95% purity by nonspecific esterase staining. These cells were\nincubated with cytokines, growth factors, hormones and lipids for 72 hours and screened for\n15-lipoxygenase expression by indirect immunofluorescence studies.", "words": [{"text": "Report", "x0": 237, "y0": 197, "x1": 277, "y1": 210, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "of", "x0": 282, "y0": 196, "x1": 294, "y1": 207, "conf": 93.0}, {"text": "Progress~Grant", "x0": 299, "y0": 196, "x1": 395, "y1": 210, "conf": 63.0}, {"text": "2489A", "x0": 400, "y0": 196, "x1": 442, "y1": 207, "conf": 81.0}, {"text": "Elliott", "x0": 324, "y0": 226, "x1": 359, "y1": 237, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "Sigal,", "x0": 364, "y0": 226, "x1": 395, "y1": 241, "conf": 93.0}, {"text": "P.l.", "x0": 401, "y0": 227, "x1": 418, "y1": 237, "conf": 1.0}, {"text": "15-Lipoxygenase", "x0": 151, "y0": 288, "x1": 251, "y1": 301, "conf": 85.0}, {"text": "is", "x0": 256, "y0": 291, "x1": 265, "y1": 298, "conf": 94.0}, {"text": "a", "x0": 271, "y0": 291, "x1": 277, "y1": 298, "conf": 94.0}, {"text": "highly", "x0": 283, "y0": 287, "x1": 317, "y1": 301, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "regulated", "x0": 323, "y0": 288, "x1": 378, "y1": 301, "conf": 9